/* Mock Exam 1: 160 new questions, weighted like the pilot exam (D1 16, D2 27, D3 31, D4 29, D5 30, D6 27). */
module.exports = { questions: [
  { m: '1.1', q: 'An insurer wants a model that estimates the repair cost of each new claim, in pounds. Which type of machine learning task is this?',
    o: ['Classification, which assigns each input to a category', 'Regression, which predicts a continuous numeric value for each input', 'Clustering, which groups similar inputs together without any labels', 'Generation, which produces new content resembling its training data'], a: 1,
    why: 'Predicting a continuous quantity, such as a cost, is regression. Classification predicts categories, and clustering finds groups without labels.' },
  { m: '1.1', q: 'A security analyst wants to find unusual login patterns but has no labelled examples of attacks. Which approach is MOST suitable?',
    o: ['Supervised classification, which first needs examples labelled as attacks', 'Unsupervised anomaly detection, which finds activity that differs from the norm', 'A language model fine-tuned only on the company’s public product documentation', 'Reinforcement learning that rewards the model for blocking every single login'], a: 1,
    why: 'Without labels, unsupervised methods find patterns and outliers. Supervised learning needs labelled attacks, and the other options don’t address the problem.' },
  { m: '1.1', q: 'Cloud spending on a company’s GPU training account triples overnight, although no new training jobs were approved. What is the MOST likely explanation to investigate?',
    o: ['A scheduled model evaluation that ran for longer than usual overnight', 'Stolen credentials used to run unauthorised workloads, such as mining', 'A GPU driver update that now reports usage in a different unit', 'Routine price rises by the provider during a period of high demand'], a: 1,
    why: 'GPU capacity is expensive and easy to monetise, so stolen cloud credentials are often used to run cryptomining or other unauthorised jobs. A sudden, unexplained spike in spend is a classic sign and should be investigated as a possible compromise.' },
  { m: '1.1', q: 'A team adapts a large pre-trained model to classify its own support tickets, using only a few thousand labelled examples. Which idea makes this possible?',
    o: ['Reinforcement learning, which rewards the model for each correct action', 'Transfer learning, which reuses what the model learned earlier', 'Unsupervised clustering, which groups the tickets without labels', 'Symbolic rules, which hard-code every ticket category by hand'], a: 1,
    why: 'Transfer learning reuses general knowledge learned during pre-training, so adapting to a new task needs far less data and compute than training from scratch.' },
  { m: '1.1', q: 'A fraud model performs poorly on its own training data and on new transactions alike. What is the MOST likely explanation?',
    o: ['Overfitting, because it has memorised its training data too closely', 'Underfitting, because it is too simple to capture the patterns', 'Data poisoning, because an attacker has corrupted every record', 'Model collapse, because it trained on its own earlier outputs'], a: 1,
    why: 'Poor performance on both training and new data points to underfitting. Overfitting shows low training error but much higher error on new data.' },
  { m: '1.1', q: 'An organisation describes its AI as a supply chain of data, models, tools and services from many parties. Why does this view matter for security?',
    o: ['It means the organisation is no longer responsible for any of its AI risks', 'Each hand-off between parties is a trust boundary that needs its own controls', 'It shows that only the final model provider ever needs a security review', 'It removes the need to keep an inventory of the AI systems in use'], a: 1,
    why: 'Every hand-off, from data sources to model providers to tools, is a point where trust must be verified. Accountability stays with the organisation.' },
  { m: '1.2', q: 'Staff ask a general-purpose LLM about a company policy introduced last month. It answers confidently, but wrongly. What is the MOST likely cause?',
    o: ['The temperature is set much too low for questions about recent events', 'The policy is newer than its training cut-off and isn’t retrieved', 'A competitor poisoned the model during its pre-training phase', 'The request exceeded the context window, so the policy was cut'], a: 1,
    why: 'A model’s knowledge stops at its training cut-off and excludes internal documents. Without retrieval, it may fill the gap with plausible but wrong answers.' },
  { m: '1.2', q: 'A RAG assistant finds a passage about “annual leave” when a user asks about “holiday entitlement”. What makes this possible?',
    o: ['Keyword matching, which looks for the exact words in the question', 'Embeddings, which place texts with similar meanings close together', 'Fine-tuning, which taught the model every policy by heart', 'Named entity recognition, which labels the people in each passage'], a: 1,
    why: 'Embeddings map text to vectors so that similar meanings sit close together, enabling semantic search beyond exact keyword matches.' },
  { m: '1.2', q: 'An assistant answers questions about internal policies by looking up the relevant policy text when each question arrives, and giving it to the model with the question. Which architecture is this?',
    o: ['Fine-tuning, which retrains the model on every policy document each night', 'Retrieval-augmented generation, which adds retrieved passages to the prompt', 'Reinforcement learning from human feedback, which ranks answers by preference', 'Transfer learning, which copies a model trained by another organisation'], a: 1,
    why: 'RAG retrieves relevant content at question time and places it in the model’s context, so answers can draw on current documents without retraining.' },
  { m: '1.2', q: 'A team is upgrading a customer service chatbot into an agent that can issue refunds and update orders through tools. Which change to the risk assessment is MOST important?',
    o: ['Re-rating the chatbot’s hallucination risk on the same basis as before', 'Treating manipulated outputs as able to trigger actions with real effects', 'Lowering the risk rating, since tools make the assistant more accurate', 'Focusing on response time, since each tool call slows down the answers'], a: 1,
    why: 'Once an assistant can act through tools, a manipulated or mistaken output no longer just produces bad text: it can move money or change records. The assessment must cover tool permissions, approvals and injection paths.' },
  { m: '1.2', q: 'A vendor markets its open-weight model as “70B”. When planning to self-host it, what does this figure MOST likely describe?',
    o: ['It was trained on about 70 billion documents from across the public web', 'It has about 70 billion parameters, the weights learned in training', 'It can accept about 70 billion tokens in one request at a time', 'It needs about 70 billion bytes of memory for every user request'], a: 1,
    why: 'The figure refers to parameters, the learned weights. Parameter count drives memory and compute needs: at 16-bit precision, each weight takes two bytes.' },
  { m: '1.2', q: 'A team wants an assistant to analyse a 600-page manual in a single request. Which model property limits this MOST directly?',
    o: ['Its temperature, which controls how random the sampling is', 'Its context window, the most tokens it can process at once', 'Its embedding size, which the vector database sets for it', 'Its training cut-off, which fixes the latest date of its knowledge'], a: 1,
    why: 'The context window caps how many tokens of input and output a model can process at once. Long documents may need chunking or retrieval.' },
  { m: '1.3', q: 'An AI malware detector catches 95% of malware, but raises so many false alarms that analysts have started ignoring it. Which metric needs improving?',
    o: ['Recall, since too much malware is still getting past the detector', 'Precision, since too many of its alerts turn out to be false positives', 'Latency, since alerts are reaching the analysts too slowly to act', 'Perplexity, since the detector’s language model is easily confused'], a: 1,
    why: 'Precision measures how many alerts are real. High recall with low precision floods analysts with false positives, leading to alert fatigue.' },
  { m: '1.3', q: 'A demand forecasting model stays uncertain even after years more data are added, because customer behaviour is inherently random. Which type of uncertainty is this?',
    o: ['Epistemic uncertainty, which shrinks as more data is collected', 'Aleatoric uncertainty, which comes from randomness in the data', 'Model drift, which comes from changing deployment conditions', 'Calibration error, which comes from overconfident probabilities'], a: 1,
    why: 'Aleatoric uncertainty reflects irreducible randomness in the data. Epistemic uncertainty comes from limited knowledge, and more data can reduce it.' },
  { m: '1.3', q: 'A summary of a security report adds a breach date that appears nowhere in the report and can’t be verified elsewhere. Which type of hallucination is this?',
    o: ['Intrinsic, because it contradicts a fact stated in the source', 'Extrinsic, because it adds content the source doesn’t support', 'A retrieval failure, because the report was missing from the index', 'Overfitting, because the model memorised other security reports'], a: 1,
    why: 'Extrinsic hallucinations add content the source doesn’t support; intrinsic ones contradict the source. Both call for grounding and verification.' },
  { m: '1.3', q: 'A model reports 90% confidence on predictions that turn out to be right only 60% of the time. What does this show?',
    o: ['High precision, since most of its confident predictions are right', 'Poor calibration, since its certainty runs ahead of its accuracy', 'Concept drift, since the meaning of its labels has changed', 'Low recall, since it misses most of the cases it should find'], a: 1,
    why: 'A well-calibrated model that says 90% is right about 90% of the time. Overconfident scores mislead reviewers and automated decisions.' },
  { m: '2.1', q: 'A team starting an AI project documents the system’s purpose, users, context and potential harms before measuring anything. Which NIST AI RMF function is this?',
    o: ['Govern, which sets culture, policies and accountability across functions', 'Map, which frames the system’s setting and identifies the risks in it', 'Measure, which analyses, assesses and tracks the identified risks', 'Manage, which prioritises the risks and decides how to act on them'], a: 1,
    why: 'Map establishes context and identifies risks. Measure assesses them, Manage treats them, and Govern is cross-cutting.' },
  { m: '2.1', q: 'Under the EU AI Act, which risk tier MOST likely applies to an AI-based email spam filter?',
    o: ['Unacceptable risk, so the system is banned in the EU', 'Minimal risk, with no specific obligations under the Act', 'High risk, needing a conformity assessment before use', 'Limited risk, needing users to be told they face AI'], a: 1,
    why: 'Most AI, such as spam filters, is minimal risk with no specific obligations under the Act, although voluntary codes of conduct are encouraged.' },
  { m: '2.1', q: 'The AI policy already requires approval for new AI tools, but staff keep asking how to get it, and each department follows a different process. Which document would BEST fix this?',
    o: ['A policy statement restating the organisation’s overall AI principles', 'A procedure setting out each step, its owner and the evidence required', 'A guideline offering optional tips for evaluating AI tools informally', 'A standard listing the encryption algorithms approved for AI services'], a: 1,
    why: 'The policy already sets the requirement; what is missing is the how. A procedure gives the step-by-step process, owners and evidence, so every department follows the same route. Guidelines are optional, and standards set specific mandatory technical requirements.' },
  { m: '2.1', q: 'In the three lines model, which function provides independent assurance over AI risk management?',
    o: ['The first line, which owns and manages AI risks daily', 'The third line, internal audit, which reports to the board', 'The second line, which sets policy and oversees the risks', 'External vendors, who supply many of the AI models in use'], a: 1,
    why: 'Internal audit, the third line, gives independent assurance to the board. The first line owns risk, and the second line oversees it.' },
  { m: '2.1', q: 'An organisation is forming an AI governance committee. Which membership is MOST appropriate?',
    o: ['Data scientists only, since they understand the models best', 'Business, legal, risk, privacy, security and technical leads', 'External vendors, since they supply most of the AI it uses', 'The marketing team, since it manages public perception of AI'], a: 1,
    why: 'AI risks cut across functions, so oversight needs business owners together with legal, risk, privacy, security and technical expertise.' },
  { m: '2.2', q: 'A regulator asks whether a hiring tool selects applicants from each group at similar rates, regardless of qualifications. Which fairness metric is this?',
    o: ['Equal opportunity, which compares true positive rates across groups', 'Demographic parity, which compares selection rates across groups', 'Calibration, which compares how accurate the scores are by group', 'Individual fairness, which compares outcomes for similar people'], a: 1,
    why: 'Demographic parity compares selection rates across groups. Equal opportunity compares true positive rates among qualified people.' },
  { m: '2.2', q: 'An organisation names a senior owner who must answer for each AI system’s outcomes and act on problems. Which principle does this MOST directly serve?',
    o: ['Transparency, telling people when and how AI is being used', 'Accountability, tying decisions to a responsible individual', 'Inclusiveness, making AI work well for diverse people', 'Robustness, keeping performance stable under stress'], a: 1,
    why: 'Accountability means identifiable people answer for AI outcomes and have the authority to act when problems arise.' },
  { m: '2.2', q: 'Two fairness metrics give conflicting results for a loan model. Who should decide which metric the organisation uses?',
    o: ['The data scientist who built the model, as a purely technical decision', 'Accountable business and governance owners, as a documented policy choice', 'The model itself, by optimising for whichever metric is easiest to meet', 'The vendor of the fairness toolkit, through its default settings'], a: 1,
    why: 'Fairness metrics can conflict mathematically, so choosing one is a value judgement that accountable owners must make and document.' },
  { m: '2.2', q: 'A risk committee wants to know which factors drive a credit model’s decisions across all applicants. Which kind of explanation fits BEST?',
    o: ['A counterfactual for one applicant who was recently declined', 'A global explanation of the model’s overall feature importance', 'The model’s raw weights, published for the committee to inspect', 'A local explanation of the most recent decision the model made'], a: 1,
    why: 'Global explanations describe overall model behaviour. Local explanations and counterfactuals explain individual decisions.' },
  { m: '2.3', q: 'A model-theft incident would cost £800,000 and is expected once every eight years. What is its annualised loss expectancy?',
    o: ['£6,400,000, multiplying the loss by the eight years', '£100,000, multiplying the single loss by an ARO of 0.125', '£800,000, since the single loss applies in every year', '£80,000, multiplying the single loss by an ARO of 0.1'], a: 1,
    why: 'ALE = SLE × ARO. Once every eight years gives an ARO of 0.125, so £800,000 × 0.125 = £100,000.' },
  { m: '2.3', q: 'Which characteristic MOST increases the risk tier of an AI agent?',
    o: ['It uses a model with more parameters than the organisation’s other models', 'It can take irreversible actions on its own, such as making payments', 'It was built by an internal team rather than bought from a vendor', 'It can produce its answers in more than one language'], a: 1,
    why: 'Autonomy over irreversible, consequential actions raises impact and blast radius, which drives the risk tier.' },
  { m: '2.3', q: 'After mitigation, an AI system’s residual risk is still above the organisation’s risk appetite. What should happen?',
    o: ['Launch anyway, and record the risk for review at next year’s audit', 'Apply further treatment, or avoid the risk, before the system launches', 'Lower the risk appetite, so that the residual risk falls within it', 'Ask the developers to accept the risk on the business’s behalf'], a: 1,
    why: 'Risk above appetite needs further treatment or avoidance. Accepting it requires an authorised owner and must fit the appetite; changing the appetite to fit is not treatment.' },
  { m: '2.3', q: 'A retailer plans to train a model on years of customer purchase records to predict life events, such as pregnancy, for targeted offers. What should the privacy team require FIRST?',
    o: ['A penetration test of the model’s hosting environment before launch', 'A data protection impact assessment, before the processing begins', 'A customer survey about the targeted offers, run after launch', 'A model card describing the model’s accuracy for marketing sign-off'], a: 1,
    why: 'Profiling customers to infer sensitive life events is likely to be high-risk processing under the GDPR, so a DPIA is required before it starts. Testing and documentation come later and don’t replace the assessment.' },
  { m: '2.3', q: 'Which details should each entry in an AI risk register include, so that the risk is actively managed?',
    o: ['Only the date on which the risk was first identified', 'The risk, its owner, rating, treatment and review date', 'The names of every person who uses the AI system', 'The model’s architecture and its total parameter count'], a: 1,
    why: 'A useful register entry names an owner, rates the risk, records the treatment decision and sets a review date, so accountability and follow-up are clear.' },
  { m: '2.4', q: 'Developers use an AI coding assistant to write product code. Which practice BEST reduces the risk of shipping code that breaches open-source licences?',
    o: ['Ask the assistant to promise that its code is always original', 'Scan generated code for matches with licensed open-source code', 'Ban all open-source software from every one of the company’s products', 'Rely on the vendor’s marketing claims about its training data'], a: 1,
    why: 'Generated code can reproduce licensed snippets. Scanning outputs for matches, alongside review and vendor indemnities, manages the IP risk.' },
  { m: '2.4', q: 'AI assistant chat logs are due for routine deletion, but legal counsel has issued a litigation hold that covers them. What should happen?',
    o: ['Delete them on schedule, since retention policy takes priority', 'Suspend deletion of all the relevant logs until the hold is lifted', 'Delete them early, before they can be requested in the litigation', 'Copy them to a personal drive, to keep them outside the system'], a: 1,
    why: 'A legal hold overrides routine deletion for the data it covers. Deleting held data can be unlawful; holds should be precise and released when they end.' },
  { m: '2.4', q: 'An AI vendor offers to indemnify customers against copyright claims arising from its tool’s outputs. What should the customer check FIRST?',
    o: ['Nothing more, since an indemnity covers every possible claim', 'Its conditions, such as keeping the vendor’s filters switched on', 'Whether the vendor has more customers than its competitors', 'That it also covers any code that staff members write entirely by hand'], a: 1,
    why: 'Vendor IP indemnities often apply only if conditions are met, such as using built-in filters and not modifying outputs, so they must be read and followed.' },
  { m: '2.4', q: 'A company keeps every prompt and response from its AI assistant indefinitely, “in case it is useful later”. Which principle does this MOST clearly breach?',
    o: ['Accuracy, which requires personal data to be kept up to date', 'Storage limitation, which limits retention to what is needed', 'Transparency, which requires telling people about processing', 'Integrity, which requires protection against unauthorised change'], a: 1,
    why: 'Retention needs a defined purpose and period. Keeping prompts indefinitely ‘just in case’ breaches storage limitation and enlarges the impact of any breach.' },
  { m: '2.5', q: 'An AI vendor’s terms let it add new sub-processors at any time without telling customers. Why does this matter MOST?',
    o: ['Sub-processors always charge customers extra fees for each request', 'Customer data could flow to unvetted parties outside agreed controls', 'Adding new sub-processors automatically reduces the model’s accuracy', 'Sub-processors are banned from handling AI services in every country'], a: 1,
    why: 'Sub-processors handle customer data for the vendor. Customers need notice and a right to object, so data stays with vetted parties under agreed controls.' },
  { m: '2.5', q: 'An AI vendor answers “yes” to every control in a security questionnaire. What should the assessor do next?',
    o: ['Accept the answers, since vendors are legally bound by them', 'Ask for evidence, such as audit reports, tests and policies', 'Reject the vendor, since perfect answers are always dishonest', 'Send the same questionnaire again in a month, to compare'], a: 1,
    why: 'Questionnaire answers are claims. Due diligence verifies them with evidence, such as independent audit reports, test results and policies, scoped to the service.' },
  { m: '2.5', q: 'When should an organisation plan its exit from a critical AI vendor?',
    o: ['Only once the vendor formally announces that it is closing the service', 'At onboarding, with data return, certified deletion and transition', 'After the first major incident involving the vendor’s service', 'Never, since exit plans signal distrust and harm the relationship'], a: 1,
    why: 'Exit is planned from day one: data and models returned in usable form, deletion certified and a transition path agreed, so the organisation isn’t locked in.' },
  { m: '2.5', q: 'An AI vendor passed a thorough due diligence review at onboarding. What should happen for the rest of the contract?',
    o: ['Nothing further, since the vendor has already been fully approved', 'Ongoing monitoring of model changes, incidents and service performance', 'A full repeat of the onboarding review every month, regardless of risk', 'Monitoring of the vendor’s invoices for unexpected price rises only'], a: 1,
    why: 'Vendor risk changes over time. Ongoing monitoring of model and sub-processor changes, incidents and performance keeps assurance current.' },
  { m: '2.6', q: 'A supervisor pauses an agent’s batch of outgoing payments to adjust its settings, then lets it continue. Which decision authority is being used?',
    o: ['Override, replacing one of the AI’s outputs with a human decision', 'Intervention, steering a process while it is still running', 'Sign-off, giving formal approval at a release or change gate', 'Stop, halting the whole system immediately and safely'], a: 1,
    why: 'Intervention pauses, adjusts or redirects a running process. Override replaces a specific output, sign-off approves at gates, and stop halts the system.' },
  { m: '2.6', q: 'A newsroom relies only on C2PA content credentials to spot AI-generated images sent in by the public. What is the main weakness?',
    o: ['C2PA credentials can only be read by the same camera that made them', 'Metadata is often stripped when images are copied or re-encoded', 'C2PA works only for text, and can’t describe images or video', 'Content credentials always reveal the photographer’s address'], a: 1,
    why: 'Provenance metadata is valuable but easily lost. A missing credential proves nothing, so combine it with watermark detection and other checks.' },
  { m: '2.6', q: 'An external auditor has been asked to review an AI system. Which evidence is MOST useful?',
    o: ['Marketing material that describes the system’s benefits to customers', 'Model cards, impact assessments, decision logs and test results', 'A list of the developers’ names, job titles and qualifications', 'Screenshots of the system’s user interface during normal use'], a: 1,
    why: 'Audit relies on documented evidence of design, assessment, decisions and testing, not on marketing or appearance.' },
  { m: '2.7', q: 'An organisation measures whether its AI awareness programme has reduced the number of AI-related data incidents. Which Kirkpatrick level is this?',
    o: ['Level 1, Reaction: how people felt about the training', 'Level 4, Results: the effect on the organisation’s outcomes', 'Level 2, Learning: what participants know after training', 'Level 3, Behaviour: whether people apply it in their work'], a: 1,
    why: 'Level 4 measures organisational results, such as fewer incidents. Level 3 measures behaviour change; levels 1 and 2 measure reaction and learning.' },
  { m: '2.7', q: 'Finance staff could be targeted by deepfake voice calls requesting urgent payments. Which training approach is MOST effective?',
    o: ['An annual policy document that staff confirm they have read', 'Realistic simulations, with coaching and a clear verification step', 'A general lecture on how neural networks generate synthetic audio', 'A poster campaign reminding staff that AI is a powerful technology'], a: 1,
    why: 'Scenario-based simulations with immediate feedback and a clear verification procedure, such as a call-back, change behaviour more than passive content.' },
  { m: '3.1', q: 'Three datasets are each harmless on their own, but a model trained on all of them can infer who individual people are. What is this called?',
    o: ['Label persistence, where classifications carry over into derived data', 'The mosaic effect, where combined data reveals what each part hides', 'Data localisation, where data must remain within national borders', 'Chain of custody, where each transfer of the data is recorded'], a: 1,
    why: 'The mosaic effect occurs when combining datasets reveals sensitive information that none reveals alone, so combined data may need a higher classification.' },
  { m: '3.1', q: 'A dataset’s business owner decides who may use it and for what. Its backups have been failing for weeks, and nobody has acted. Who is MOST directly responsible for fixing the backups?',
    o: ['The data owner, who must personally carry out all technical work on it', 'The data custodian, who operates the storage and its technical controls', 'The data protection officer, who monitors compliance with privacy law', 'The data scientists, who are the main users of the training dataset'], a: 1,
    why: 'Custodians operate the storage, backups and technical controls on the owner’s behalf. The owner stays accountable and sets requirements but doesn’t run the systems, and the DPO advises and monitors.' },
  { m: '3.1', q: 'A company hires a vendor to label customer images on its behalf, following its instructions. Which document should govern the vendor’s processing?',
    o: ['A non-disclosure agreement covering only the vendor’s staff', 'A data processing agreement setting purpose, security and limits', 'A public privacy notice published on the company’s main website', 'A service level agreement covering only labelling turnaround'], a: 1,
    why: 'A processor acting on the controller’s instructions needs a data processing agreement setting out purpose, instructions, security, sub-processors and deletion.' },
  { m: '3.1', q: 'A UK bank requires its AI provider to store all customer data in UK data centres. The provider’s parent company is based abroad, under rules that let local authorities demand information held anywhere by its subsidiaries. Which concern does the residency requirement MOST clearly leave unaddressed?',
    o: ['Availability, since hosting in one country rules out any redundancy', 'Sovereignty, since another country’s laws may still apply to the data', 'Retention, since UK law requires all AI training data to be deleted yearly', 'Portability, since UK-hosted data can’t legally move to another provider'], a: 1,
    why: 'Residency controls where data sits; sovereignty concerns whose laws apply. A provider subject to foreign law may be compelled to hand over data it controls, wherever it is stored. Contracts, customer-held keys and sovereign cloud options address this.' },
  { m: '3.1', q: 'An EU company wants an AI provider in a country without an adequacy decision to process its customers’ personal data. Which mechanism is commonly used?',
    o: ['A verbal assurance from the provider’s sales team that data is safe', 'Standard contractual clauses, with a transfer risk assessment', 'Encrypting the data with keys that the provider itself holds', 'A brief mention of the transfer in the company’s privacy notice'], a: 1,
    why: 'Transfers to countries without adequacy need a safeguard, typically standard contractual clauses, supported by a transfer risk assessment and any supplementary measures.' },
  { m: '3.2', q: 'Before publishing a de-identified dataset, a UK organisation asks whether a determined person, without specialist skills, could re-identify anyone. What is this test called?',
    o: ['The k-anonymity check, counting identical records in each group', 'The motivated intruder test, a key benchmark for anonymisation', 'The membership inference test, run against a trained model', 'The minimum necessary standard, taken from US health law'], a: 1,
    why: 'The motivated intruder test asks whether a reasonably competent, determined person using available resources could re-identify individuals.' },
  { m: '3.2', q: 'A mobile app needs usage statistics, but the company must never see users’ raw values. Which privacy technique fits BEST?',
    o: ['Central differential privacy, adding noise to results on the server', 'Local differential privacy, adding noise on each device before collection', 'Pseudonymisation, replacing user names with random IDs on the server', 'Encryption at rest, protecting the raw values once they are stored'], a: 1,
    why: 'Local differential privacy adds noise on the device, so the collector never receives true individual values. Central DP trusts the server with raw data.' },
  { m: '3.2', q: 'An attacker who knows some of a person’s details uses a model’s outputs to deduce their health condition. What is this attack?',
    o: ['Membership inference, revealing whether a record was in training', 'Attribute inference, deducing a sensitive trait about a person', 'Model extraction, copying the model’s behaviour into a substitute', 'Data poisoning, corrupting the model through its training data'], a: 1,
    why: 'Attribute inference uses model outputs and known details to infer sensitive attributes. Membership inference reveals whether a record was in the training data.' },
  { m: '3.2', q: 'A data scientist finds that a fraud model’s training extract includes card security codes (CVV2) copied from payment processing logs. What does PCI DSS require?',
    o: ['Keep them, as long as the training extract is encrypted at rest', 'Remove them, as they mustn’t be stored after authorisation', 'Keep them, as long as the card numbers are pseudonymised', 'Remove them only if model accuracy doesn’t depend on them'], a: 1,
    why: 'Sensitive authentication data, such as card security codes, must not be stored after authorisation, even if encrypted, so it can’t be used for training.' },
  { m: '3.2', q: 'Which training approach limits each record’s influence to one part of the model, so that honouring a deletion request needs only that part to be retrained?',
    o: ['Federated learning, which trains across separate devices', 'SISA training, which isolates data into separate shards', 'Differential privacy, which adds noise during training', 'Transfer learning, which reuses a pre-trained model'], a: 1,
    why: 'SISA (sharded, isolated, sliced, aggregated) training limits each record’s influence to one shard, so removing it needs only that shard retrained.' },
  { m: '3.3', q: 'Attackers add correctly labelled but subtly altered images to a training set, so that the model later misclassifies one target. Why won’t a review of labels catch this?',
    o: ['Label reviews only ever check a tiny random sample of the data', 'The labels are correct; the poison is in the content itself', 'Altered images are always removed by normal data augmentation', 'Image models don’t use labels at all during their training'], a: 1,
    why: 'Clean-label poisoning keeps labels correct and hides the attack in the content, so label checks miss it. Provenance and data validation are needed.' },
  { m: '3.3', q: 'After a poisoning incident, investigators can’t tell whether approved training snapshots were altered, because administrators could edit them. Which control would BEST prevent this in future?',
    o: ['Weekly manual reviews of a random sample of the stored training files', 'Immutable write-once storage, with retention locks for a fixed period', 'Faster storage, so that snapshots can be recreated from source quickly', 'Longer passwords for the administrators who manage the storage system'], a: 1,
    why: 'Write-once (WORM) storage with retention locks stops anyone, including administrators, from changing or deleting approved snapshots for a set period, so their integrity can be trusted. Sampling, speed and passwords don’t give that guarantee.' },
  { m: '3.3', q: 'A model scores 98% in testing, but many test records were also in its training data. What does this MOST likely mean?',
    o: ['The model is ready for production, since 98% beats any benchmark', 'Train–test leakage has inflated the score, overstating real performance', 'The test set is too small, but the overlap itself doesn’t matter', 'The model has underfitted, since it saw the test data beforehand'], a: 1,
    why: 'Train–test leakage lets a model be scored on data it has already seen, inflating results. Test sets must be kept strictly separate.' },
  { m: '3.3', q: 'Two annotators label the same set of images and agree on only 55% of them. What does this MOST likely indicate?',
    o: ['Both annotators are deliberately poisoning the labels together', 'Unclear guidelines or unreliable labels, needing review first', 'The images are too high in resolution for annotators to judge', 'The labels are fine, since agreement is never above 60%'], a: 1,
    why: 'Low inter-annotator agreement suggests ambiguous guidelines, poor training or bad labels. Investigate before the data is used.' },
  { m: '3.3', q: 'An attacker uses a face recognition model’s outputs to reconstruct a recognisable image of someone in its training data. What is this attack?',
    o: ['Membership inference, revealing if a face was in training', 'Model inversion, recovering likenesses of people it learned from', 'Evasion, altering a face so that the model misidentifies it', 'Model extraction, copying the model’s behaviour via queries'], a: 1,
    why: 'Model inversion reconstructs representative training data, such as faces, from a model’s outputs or confidence scores.' },
  { m: '3.4', q: 'A genomics company stores encrypted training data that must stay confidential for at least 30 years. Which cryptographic action is MOST important to plan for?',
    o: ['Replacing AES-256, since quantum computers will break it outright', 'Migrating key establishment to post-quantum algorithms, such as ML-KEM', 'Compressing the data before encryption, so less of it can be harvested', 'Replacing encryption with hashing, since hashes can never be reversed'], a: 1,
    why: 'Data captured today could be decrypted later if its key exchange relies on RSA or elliptic curves, which large quantum computers could break. Moving to post-quantum key establishment such as ML-KEM (FIPS 203) protects long-lived data. AES-256 is still considered strong.' },
  { m: '3.4', q: 'A bank’s DLP flags thousands of prompts a day because they contain any 16-digit number, and analysts can’t spot actual leaks among the false alarms. Which change BEST cuts the noise while still catching genuine leaks?',
    o: ['Turning off DLP for prompts, since AI tools already filter sensitive data', 'Exact data match, using fingerprints of the bank’s real customer records', 'Flagging only prompts that contain five or more 16-digit numbers', 'Blocking every prompt that contains any digits, whatever the context'], a: 1,
    why: 'Exact data match compares content against hashed fingerprints of the organisation’s actual records, so it flags real customer data rather than anything that looks like a card number. Disabling DLP, crude thresholds or blocking all digits are far worse trade-offs.' },
  { m: '3.4', q: 'A cloud analytics service must run calculations on customer data without ever decrypting it. Which technique makes this possible?',
    o: ['Tokenisation, which swaps sensitive values for random tokens', 'Homomorphic encryption, which computes directly on ciphertext', 'Envelope encryption, which wraps data keys with a master key', 'Transport encryption, which protects data moving over networks'], a: 1,
    why: 'Homomorphic encryption lets computation happen on encrypted data, at a high performance cost. The other options protect data at rest, in transit or by substitution.' },
  { m: '3.4', q: 'An AI support tool should show most staff only the last four digits of card numbers, without changing the stored data. Which technique fits?',
    o: ['Static masking, which permanently overwrites the stored values', 'Dynamic data masking, applied whenever the data is queried', 'Cryptographic erase, which destroys the encryption keys', 'Hashing, which replaces each value with a one-way digest'], a: 1,
    why: 'Dynamic data masking hides sensitive values at query time based on the user’s role, leaving stored data unchanged for authorised uses.' },
  { m: '3.5', q: 'A credit model’s developers want to sign off its validation themselves, to meet a launch date. What is the BEST response from the model risk manager?',
    o: ['Accept their sign-off, since the developers understand the model best', 'Require validation by a qualified party independent of the build team', 'Skip validation for now, and validate at the first annual model review', 'Ask the developers to double the number of tests they run themselves'], a: 1,
    why: 'Independent validation provides effective challenge, free of the developers’ assumptions and incentives. It is a core principle of model risk management, as in SR 11-7. More self-testing doesn’t remove the conflict of interest.' },
  { m: '3.5', q: 'How does a hash chain make an AI system’s audit log tamper-evident?',
    o: ['Each entry is encrypted with a different key held by the auditors', 'Each entry includes the previous entry’s hash, exposing later edits', 'The whole log is compressed into one file each night for storage', 'Entries are written by the model itself, so no one else can edit them'], a: 1,
    why: 'Linking each entry to the previous one’s hash means any change or deletion breaks every later link, making tampering detectable.' },
  { m: '3.5', q: 'A document-drafting assistant returns Office files that staff open directly. Which control removes macros and other active content before files reach users?',
    o: ['Encryption, which stops unauthorised people from reading the files', 'Content disarm and reconstruction, rebuilding clean copies of files', 'Watermarking, which marks each file as having been made by the assistant', 'Compression, which reduces each file’s size before it is sent'], a: 1,
    why: 'Content disarm and reconstruction strips active elements, such as macros and scripts, and rebuilds files with only known-safe content.' },
  { m: '3.5', q: 'A provider’s high-risk AI system, on the EU market for a year, has begun making more errors for one group of users. Which provider obligation under the AI Act is MOST directly meant to catch and act on this?',
    o: ['A fresh conformity assessment before anyone may keep using the system', 'Post-market monitoring, collecting and analysing performance in use', 'A fundamental rights impact assessment, carried out by the provider', 'Registration of the issue in an EU database of general-purpose models'], a: 1,
    why: 'Providers of high-risk systems must run a post-market monitoring system that collects and analyses data on performance in use, so emerging problems are found and corrected. Fundamental rights impact assessments fall to certain deployers, and general-purpose models are a separate regime.' },
  { m: '3.6', q: 'What does keyless signing, as used by Sigstore, bind a model’s signature to?',
    o: ['A long-lived private key stored on the developer’s own laptop', 'A short-lived certificate for a verified identity, logged publicly', 'The model’s file size and the exact date when it was first uploaded', 'A shared password known to everyone in the model publishing team'], a: 1,
    why: 'Keyless signing issues short-lived certificates tied to an authenticated identity and records signatures in a public transparency log, avoiding long-lived keys.' },
  { m: '3.6', q: 'A provider wants to detect whether text circulating online was generated by its own model. Which technique fits BEST?',
    o: ['A model watermark that responds to a secret set of trigger inputs', 'An output watermark embedded statistically in what it writes', 'A digital signature applied to the model’s weight files', 'A per-licensee fingerprint in each customer’s copy of the model'], a: 1,
    why: 'Output watermarks mark generated content itself, so text can be tested later. Model watermarks and fingerprints identify copies of the model, not its outputs.' },
  { m: '3.6', q: 'A model passed integrity checks at the deployment gate. Weeks later, investigators find its file in storage was swapped, and the serving system ran the swapped file without noticing. Which control would BEST have prevented this?',
    o: ['Running the deployment gate checks more often, such as every night', 'Verifying the model’s signature again each time the file is loaded', 'Encrypting the storage volume that holds the model files at rest', 'Logging every model load, so that swaps can be found during audits'], a: 1,
    why: 'Verification at the gate only proves the file was good at that moment. Verifying the signature at load time catches files altered or swapped in storage afterwards. Nightly checks leave gaps, encryption at rest doesn’t stop tampering through authorised access, and logs only help after the fact.' },
  { m: '3.6', q: 'A customer-facing AI API must allow short bursts, such as a user pasting several documents at once, while holding each user to a steady average rate. Which rate-limiting design fits BEST?',
    o: ['A fixed window that resets the count at the start of every minute', 'A token bucket that refills continuously, up to a maximum burst size', 'A hard limit of one request per second, with no allowance for bursts', 'A monthly quota, with no limit on how quickly requests can be made'], a: 1,
    why: 'A token bucket lets a client spend saved-up tokens in a burst, up to the bucket size, while the refill rate enforces the long-run average. Fixed windows allow double bursts at window edges, and quotas don’t control the rate.' },
  { m: '3.7', q: 'Attackers edit public web pages just before a dataset’s scheduled snapshot is taken, then revert them. What is this attack?',
    o: ['Split-view poisoning, changing content after a URL list is curated', 'Frontrunning poisoning, exploiting predictable collection times', 'Typosquatting, registering names that look like popular ones', 'Dependency confusion, publishing a public package over a private one'], a: 1,
    why: 'Frontrunning poisoning times malicious edits to be captured in a periodic snapshot, then reverts them so reviewers see clean pages.' },
  { m: '3.7', q: 'A developer’s general-purpose AI model was trained using more than 10²⁵ floating-point operations of compute. Under the EU AI Act, what MOST likely follows?',
    o: ['The model is automatically banned from being offered in the EU market', 'It is presumed to pose systemic risk, adding duties such as adversarial testing', 'It must be registered as a high-risk AI system under Annex III of the Act', 'It is exempt from transparency duties because of its scale and capability'], a: 1,
    why: 'Training compute above 10²⁵ FLOPs creates a presumption of high-impact capabilities, so the model is treated as a general-purpose AI model with systemic risk. Its provider must evaluate and adversarially test it, assess and mitigate systemic risks, report serious incidents and ensure cyber security.' },
  { m: '3.7', q: 'A data scientist installs a package named “pytorch-utilz”, mistyping a popular library’s name, and it steals cloud credentials. What attack is this?',
    o: ['Dependency confusion, preferring a public package over a private one', 'Typosquatting, registering look-alike names to catch slips', 'Model namespace reuse, re-registering a deleted publisher’s name', 'Frontrunning, timing malicious edits to a dataset snapshot'], a: 1,
    why: 'Typosquatting relies on look-alike names catching typing mistakes. Pinned, allow-listed dependencies from internal mirrors reduce the risk.' },
  { m: '3.7', q: 'A model hub shows a “verified publisher” badge on a model’s page. What does this badge tell you?',
    o: ['The model has been scanned and found free of any backdoors', 'The account belongs to the organisation it claims to be', 'The hub guarantees the model’s accuracy for every use case', 'The model’s licence permits any commercial use at all'], a: 1,
    why: 'Verification confirms the publisher’s identity, not the model’s safety. Pin revisions, scan files and evaluate the model before use.' },
  { m: '4.1', q: 'An internal RAG assistant trusts any request from inside the corporate network, and any document already in its index. Which change BEST applies Zero Trust principles?',
    o: ['Moving the assistant into its own network segment, then trusting it fully', 'Verifying every user and request, and treating indexed content as untrusted', 'Adding a stronger firewall at the network perimeter around the assistant', 'Requiring a VPN connection, so that only internal users can reach it'], a: 1,
    why: 'Zero Trust removes implicit trust based on network location: every identity and request is verified, access is least privilege, and content the model reads is treated as untrusted. Segments, firewalls and VPNs alone still rely on location.' },
  { m: '4.1', q: 'An assistant fetches order details through an API that checks the user is signed in, but not that the order belongs to them. Which weakness is this?',
    o: ['Token passthrough, forwarding the client’s token to downstream APIs', 'Missing object-level authorisation on each requested record', 'Tool poisoning, hiding instructions in a tool’s description', 'A rug pull, where a tool’s behaviour changes after approval'], a: 1,
    why: 'Object-level authorisation checks that the caller may access the specific record requested. Without it, any signed-in user can fetch others’ data by changing an ID.' },
  { m: '4.1', q: 'Which control MOST directly limits an attacker’s lateral movement from a compromised inference server to the training data stores?',
    o: ['Longer and more complex passwords for every data science user', 'Micro-segmentation, with tightly controlled traffic between zones', 'A larger model, which is assumed to resist attacks more strongly', 'Annual penetration tests of the organisation’s external perimeter'], a: 1,
    why: 'Micro-segmentation divides workloads into small zones with deny-by-default traffic rules, so one compromised server can’t freely reach others.' },
  { m: '4.1', q: 'A team begins a STRIDE threat model of a new RAG assistant. Which artefact should it usually create first?',
    o: ['A marketing plan describing how and when the assistant will be launched', 'A data flow diagram of components, stores, flows and boundaries', 'A list of every possible prompt that users might send to it', 'The final penetration test report from the previous system'], a: 1,
    why: 'Threat modelling starts from a data flow diagram showing components, data stores, external entities, flows and trust boundaries, to which STRIDE is applied.' },
  { m: '4.1', q: 'An architecture decision record documents why a team chose not to log full prompts. What is its MAIN value?',
    o: ['It replaces the need for any privacy impact assessment', 'It records the reasoning and residual risk for later review', 'It guarantees that the decision complies with every law', 'It lets the team change the decision without any approval'], a: 1,
    why: 'ADRs capture context, options, reasoning and residual risk, so decisions can be reviewed and revisited when circumstances change.' },
  { m: '4.2', q: 'An assessment finds that the baseboard management controllers (BMCs) on GPU training servers are reachable from the office network and still use default passwords. What should be done FIRST?',
    o: ['Change the default passwords at the next annual maintenance window', 'Move them to an isolated management network and change the passwords', 'Block the office network’s internet access to protect the controllers', 'Encrypt the training data that is stored on the servers’ local disks'], a: 1,
    why: 'BMCs give out-of-band control below the operating system, so exposure with default credentials allows a full takeover of the hardware. Isolating them on a restricted management network and replacing the credentials is the urgent fix; firmware updates follow.' },
  { m: '4.2', q: 'Which Kubernetes baseline stops containers on an inference cluster from running privileged or as root?',
    o: ['Horizontal pod autoscaling, set with a maximum replica count', 'Pod Security Standards, enforced at the restricted level', 'Resource quotas that cap the GPUs each namespace may use', 'Node affinity rules that pin pods to particular GPU types'], a: 1,
    why: 'Pod Security Standards define baseline and restricted profiles that block privileged containers, root users and host access.' },
  { m: '4.2', q: 'A cloud GPU cluster’s bill spikes, and its GPUs show full utilisation even though no jobs are scheduled. What is the MOST likely cause?',
    o: ['A routine driver update that runs on every GPU each month', 'Cryptojacking, with attackers using the GPUs to mine currency', 'Model drift, which makes inference far more expensive over time', 'A logging misconfiguration that inflates the reported usage'], a: 1,
    why: 'Unexplained full GPU utilisation and cost spikes suggest hijacked compute, as in the ShadowRay campaign. Investigate exposed dashboards and credentials.' },
  { m: '4.2', q: 'During extreme demand, an inference service drops low-priority batch requests to keep customer-facing responses within target. What is this called?',
    o: ['Autoscaling, which adds capacity automatically as demand rises', 'Load shedding, sacrificing less important work under pressure', 'Preemption, which evicts running jobs to make way for others', 'Rate limiting, which caps how often each client may send requests'], a: 1,
    why: 'Load shedding deliberately drops or defers low-priority work under pressure, so that critical services keep meeting their targets.' },
  { m: '4.3', q: 'Model builds run on long-lived CI runners that are reused across projects. After one build ran a malicious dependency, the team fears later builds were tampered with. Which change BEST prevents this kind of persistence?',
    o: ['Scanning every runner for malware once a month, then cleaning it', 'Using a fresh, ephemeral runner for each build, destroyed afterwards', 'Giving each project’s runner a longer and stronger admin password', 'Running builds only at night, when fewer engineers are working'], a: 1,
    why: 'Ephemeral runners start clean for every job and are destroyed afterwards, so anything an attacker plants can’t persist into later builds. Periodic scans and passwords don’t stop a compromised runner contaminating the next job.' },
  { m: '4.3', q: 'Why are distroless images often preferred for model-serving containers?',
    o: ['They run models faster by compiling them directly into the kernel', 'They omit shells and package managers, shrinking the attack surface', 'They encrypt the model weights automatically while serving', 'They allow any package to be installed at runtime when needed'], a: 1,
    why: 'Distroless images contain only the application and its runtime dependencies, leaving attackers fewer tools and fewer vulnerable packages.' },
  { m: '4.3', q: 'A training pipeline breaks whenever an upstream team renames fields in a data feed. Which practice BEST prevents this?',
    o: ['Allowing the pipeline to guess the meaning of any new field names', 'A data contract agreeing the feed’s schema, quality and ownership', 'Copying the upstream team’s database into the pipeline each night', 'Turning off schema validation so that the pipeline never fails'], a: 1,
    why: 'A data contract sets out a feed’s schema, quality rules, meaning and ownership, so changes are agreed and checked rather than silently breaking consumers.' },
  { m: '4.3', q: 'An AI release pipeline checks that each model passes its safety evaluation suite. A failure would expose customers to harmful outputs. Which configuration is BEST for this check?',
    o: ['A soft gate that warns the team but lets the release continue', 'A hard gate that blocks the release until the failure is resolved', 'A dashboard metric that is reviewed at the end of each quarter', 'An optional check that teams may run when they have spare time'], a: 1,
    why: 'Checks whose failure would cause unacceptable harm should be hard gates that block release. Soft gates, which warn and need sign-off to proceed, suit lower-risk checks where a documented override is acceptable.' },
  { m: '4.4', q: 'Which statement about prompt injection guardrails is MOST accurate?',
    o: ['A good enough classifier can stop every single injection attempt', 'No guardrail stops every injection, so limit what outputs can do', 'Guardrails are unnecessary if the system prompt is kept secret', 'Injection only affects models that were trained on public data'], a: 1,
    why: 'Injection can’t be filtered out completely, so systems must limit the consequences: least privilege, approvals and output validation.' },
  { m: '4.4', q: 'A model is trained to give system and developer instructions priority over user input, and user input priority over tool content. What is this?',
    o: ['Context budgeting, which reserves context space for key instructions', 'An instruction hierarchy, which ranks sources when they conflict', 'Spotlighting, which marks untrusted content in the prompt', 'Plan-then-execute, which fixes the plan before reading data'], a: 1,
    why: 'An instruction hierarchy trains models to prefer higher-privilege instructions when sources conflict. It helps, but doesn’t guarantee resistance to injection.' },
  { m: '4.4', q: 'A team deliberately cuts off its AI service’s model provider in a controlled test, to check that the fallbacks work. What is this practice?',
    o: ['Load testing, which measures performance under expected traffic', 'Chaos engineering, which injects failures to test resilience', 'Fuzzing, which sends malformed inputs to find crashes', 'Soak testing, which runs the service for long periods'], a: 1,
    why: 'Chaos engineering deliberately injects failures, such as a provider outage, to test a hypothesis about how the system behaves and recovers.' },
  { m: '4.4', q: 'Which testing technique feeds an AI application large numbers of random or malformed inputs to uncover crashes and unsafe behaviour?',
    o: ['Soak testing, which runs a steady load for many hours', 'Fuzzing, which hunts for edge-case failures automatically', 'Spike testing, which applies sudden bursts of traffic', 'Regression testing, which repeats known test cases'], a: 1,
    why: 'Fuzzing generates random or structured input variations to find crashes, parsing errors and unsafe behaviour that normal tests miss.' },
  { m: '4.5', q: 'Before an assistant lets a user approve a large payment, it asks them to re-authenticate with a passkey. What is this control?',
    o: ['Single sign-on, which signs users in once for many applications', 'Step-up authentication, requiring stronger proof for risky actions', 'SCIM provisioning, which creates and removes accounts automatically', 'Session time-out, which signs users out after a period of inactivity'], a: 1,
    why: 'Step-up authentication asks for fresh, stronger authentication before sensitive actions, limiting the damage from a hijacked session.' },
  { m: '4.5', q: 'A vault issues an agent a database credential for one task, then revokes it automatically when the task ends. What is this?',
    o: ['A static secret, rotated manually by an administrator each year', 'A dynamic secret, created just in time and short-lived by design', 'A shared secret, used by every agent that connects to the database', 'A hard-coded secret, compiled into the agent’s container image'], a: 1,
    why: 'Dynamic secrets are generated on demand for one task or session and revoked afterwards, so a stolen credential has little lasting value.' },
  { m: '4.5', q: 'Engineers sometimes sign in to the company’s AI platform from personal, unmanaged laptops in public places. Which identity control BEST limits this risk without blocking legitimate work?',
    o: ['Banning all remote access to the AI platform for every engineer', 'Conditional access, requiring a compliant device and assessing context', 'A signed policy in which engineers promise to use managed devices', 'Doubling the minimum password length for all platform accounts'], a: 1,
    why: 'Conditional access evaluates signals such as device compliance, location and risk at sign-in, and can require a managed device or step-up authentication. A ban stops legitimate work, and promises or passwords don’t enforce anything about the device.' },
  { m: '4.5', q: 'An attacker with a stolen password sends an ML engineer repeated sign-in push prompts until one is approved. What is this, and the BEST defence?',
    o: ['Credential stuffing, defended by longer and more complex passwords', 'MFA fatigue, defended by phishing-resistant methods such as passkeys', 'Password spraying, defended by locking every account after one failure', 'Session hijacking, defended by shorter session cookie lifetimes'], a: 1,
    why: 'MFA fatigue floods users with push requests. Phishing-resistant methods, such as passkeys, remove the one-tap approval that it exploits.' },
  { m: '4.6', q: 'An agent can read the full customer database and also send email to any external address. Each permission was approved separately. What risk do these permissions create together?',
    o: ['Excessive autonomy, since the agent acts without human approval', 'A toxic combination, letting one identity complete data theft alone', 'Configuration drift, since its permissions changed without review', 'A rug pull, since its tools changed behaviour after approval'], a: 1,
    why: 'Permissions that are acceptable alone can together let one identity complete a sensitive process, here reading and exfiltrating data. Review combinations, not just items.' },
  { m: '4.6', q: 'An agent should read contract files only during business hours, only for its user’s own region, and never files labelled “restricted”. Which access control model fits these rules BEST?',
    o: ['Role-based access control, giving the agent a single “contracts” role', 'Attribute-based access control, evaluating time, location and labels', 'Discretionary access control, letting each file’s owner decide ad hoc', 'Mandatory access control, based only on government clearance levels'], a: 1,
    why: 'Attribute-based access control decides using attributes of the subject, resource, action and environment, such as time, region and sensitivity labels. A single role can’t express these conditions without an explosion of roles.' },
  { m: '4.6', q: 'An agent’s tool calls are authorised once when its task starts, and never checked again. Which principle does this violate?',
    o: ['Defence in depth, which layers several independent controls', 'Complete mediation, which leaves no access request unverified', 'Secure by default, which ships with risky features turned off', 'Separation of duties, which splits sensitive tasks between people'], a: 1,
    why: 'Complete mediation requires every access to be checked in downstream systems, not just once, so changes in context or permission take effect.' },
  { m: '4.6', q: 'An agent only needs to read calendar availability, but its calendar plugin can also create, edit and delete events. Which weakness is this?',
    o: ['Excessive autonomy, as the agent acts without any human review', 'Excessive functionality, as the plugin exposes surplus operations', 'Excessive permissions, as the agent’s identity has administrator rights', 'Excessive cost, as the plugin uses too many tokens per request'], a: 1,
    why: 'Excessive functionality means tools offer more operations than the task needs (OWASP LLM06). Use narrow tools that expose only what is required.' },
  { m: '4.6', q: 'An agent starts an eight-hour data migration on behalf of an employee. Two hours in, the employee is dismissed and their account is disabled. Which authorisation design BEST handles this?',
    o: ['The agent finishes the task, since it was authorised when it started', 'Access is re-evaluated during the task, so the agent’s rights end too', 'The agent continues, and its logs are reviewed at the end of the month', 'The task pauses until the employee confirms they still want it to run'], a: 1,
    why: 'Authority delegated by a user should end when the user’s own access ends. Continuous evaluation, for example with short-lived tokens and revocation signals, stops the agent acting on a dismissed employee’s behalf.' },
  { m: '4.7', q: 'Every new ML engineer automatically receives write access to all training datasets on their first day. What is the MAIN risk?',
    o: ['New engineers can’t start work until each dataset is approved', 'Over-broad birthright access gives people more than they need', 'Datasets become corrupted when too many people can read them', 'Automatic access breaks the joiner process in most HR systems'], a: 1,
    why: 'Birthright access should be the minimum common to a role. Broad default write access spreads excess privilege and widens insider and compromise risk.' },
  { m: '4.7', q: 'A cloud estate has hundreds of AI workload identities, and nobody knows which of their permissions are actually used. Which tool would BEST support right-sizing them?',
    o: ['A cloud web application firewall in front of the AI services', 'A cloud infrastructure entitlement management (CIEM) platform', 'A cloud vulnerability scanner for the workloads’ containers', 'A data loss prevention policy for the workloads’ buckets'], a: 1,
    why: 'CIEM tools calculate the effective permissions of cloud identities, including workloads, and highlight permissions that are never used, so they can be removed. Firewalls, scanners and DLP address different problems.' },
  { m: '4.7', q: 'Which capability detects and responds in real time to attacks on identities themselves, such as stolen agent tokens being replayed?',
    o: ['Data security posture management, which finds and classifies stored data', 'Identity threat detection and response, a specialist SOC capability', 'Content moderation, which screens model outputs for harm', 'Vulnerability scanning, which finds missing software patches'], a: 1,
    why: 'ITDR monitors identity systems and token use for attacks such as token theft, replay and privilege escalation, and responds in real time.' },
  { m: '5.1', q: 'An engineer edits a guardrail setting directly on a production server. Within minutes, the setting reverts to the version stored in the repository. Which practice did this?',
    o: ['Load shedding, which drops low-priority work under heavy demand', 'GitOps reconciliation, restoring the approved state from Git', 'Model drift detection, which compares inputs with a baseline', 'A circuit breaker, which trips when error rates exceed a threshold'], a: 1,
    why: 'GitOps reconciliation continuously compares the running system with the desired state in version control, restoring or flagging unapproved changes.' },
  { m: '5.1', q: 'An incident team carries out its real containment procedures for a poisoned model in a test environment, rather than just discussing them. Which exercise is this?',
    o: ['A tabletop exercise, in which participants talk through a scenario', 'A functional exercise, a realistic hands-on rehearsal of the response', 'A post-implementation review, held after an emergency change', 'A red team exercise, in which testers attack the live system'], a: 1,
    why: 'Functional exercises have participants perform real procedures in a test environment. Tabletop exercises discuss responses without carrying them out.' },
  { m: '5.1', q: 'A team suspects that settings on its production AI gateway have drifted, but different engineers remember different “correct” values. What should it establish FIRST?',
    o: ['A rota of engineers who check the settings by hand every morning', 'An approved, version-controlled baseline to measure drift against', 'A new gateway product that has fewer configurable settings to manage', 'An all-staff email asking everyone not to change any settings'], a: 1,
    why: 'Drift can only be detected against a known, approved baseline of settings and versions. Once the baseline is under version control, tools can compare against it continuously and reconcile changes.' },
  { m: '5.1', q: 'A model’s accuracy has been falling for weeks. Its accountable business owner says monitoring and retraining aren’t their job. Who is MOST appropriately responsible for this day-to-day work?',
    o: ['The internal audit team, which reviews models on an annual cycle', 'The model’s technical owner, who looks after its health in production', 'The vendor that supplied the base model the system was built on', 'The data protection officer, who oversees personal data processing'], a: 1,
    why: 'The model owner stays accountable, while a technical owner looks after its day-to-day health: monitoring, fixes and retraining. Audit gives independent assurance, and the DPO advises on data protection.' },
  { m: '5.2', q: 'A SOC receives AI events from a gateway, an agent framework and a model provider, each in a different format. Which practice makes them easier to query together?',
    o: ['Storing each source’s events in a separate, unlinked archive', 'Normalising them to a common schema, such as OCSF, on arrival', 'Converting every event into a free-text summary for analysts', 'Deleting fields that don’t appear in all three of the formats'], a: 1,
    why: 'Normalising events to a vendor-neutral schema, such as OCSF, lets detections and queries work consistently across sources.' },
  { m: '5.2', q: 'Downstream fraud tools reject masked account numbers unless they still look like 16-digit numbers, and authorised staff must be able to recover the originals. Which technique fits?',
    o: ['Redaction, which replaces each value with a fixed placeholder', 'Format-preserving encryption, reversible with the right key', 'Generalisation, which replaces values with broader ranges', 'Unkeyed hashing, which turns values into fixed-length digests'], a: 1,
    why: 'Format-preserving encryption keeps the original format, so downstream tools still work, and authorised key holders can decrypt the values.' },
  { m: '5.2', q: 'During an investigation, an agent’s tool calls appear to happen before the prompts that triggered them. What is the MOST likely cause?',
    o: ['The agent predicted the prompts before users actually sent them', 'Clock skew between the systems that recorded the events', 'Log injection, inserting fake entries into the agent’s logs', 'Embedding drift, shifting the agent’s inputs over time'], a: 1,
    why: 'Clock differences between systems distort the order of events. Synchronised time and UTC timestamps prevent misleading timelines.' },
  { m: '5.2', q: 'An incident review finds that an agent platform’s logs stopped reaching the SIEM two days before the attack, and nobody noticed. Which control would BEST have revealed the problem in time?',
    o: ['A larger SIEM licence, so that more events can be stored for longer', 'Alerts on missing heartbeats and on gaps in log sequence numbers', 'A monthly report of the total events received from each source', 'Encrypting the log stream between the agent platform and the SIEM'], a: 1,
    why: 'Heartbeats and sequence numbers let the SIEM alert as soon as a source goes quiet or events go missing. A monthly report would be far too slow, and storage and encryption don’t detect silence.' },
  { m: '5.3', q: 'A security team plants a decoy API key in an agent’s configuration files. The key has no legitimate use. What is its purpose?',
    o: ['To give the agent a spare key if its normal credential expires', 'To raise an alert if anyone ever uses it, signalling compromise', 'To reduce the provider’s bill by spreading usage across two keys', 'To test whether the agent can rotate its own credentials safely'], a: 1,
    why: 'A honeytoken has no legitimate use, so any attempt to use it signals that someone has reached places they shouldn’t.' },
  { m: '5.3', q: 'An engineer’s token is used to call the model registry from London and, ten minutes later, from Singapore. What does this signal suggest?',
    o: ['A normal pattern for engineers who travel often for their work', 'Impossible travel, a strong sign of stolen and reused credentials', 'A load balancer routing traffic through different cloud regions', 'Clock skew between the registry’s servers in the two regions'], a: 1,
    why: 'Use of one identity from places too far apart for the time between them suggests credential or token theft, needing investigation and revocation.' },
  { m: '5.3', q: 'Attackers encode injected instructions in Base64 and unusual Unicode characters to slip them past an AI gateway’s filters. Which detection improvement helps MOST?',
    o: ['Scanning only inputs that are written in plain English text', 'Normalising and decoding inputs before they are scanned', 'Raising the model’s temperature to disrupt encoded commands', 'Blocking every request that contains any number at all'], a: 1,
    why: 'Input obfuscation hides malicious content in encodings and look-alike characters. Normalise and decode inputs before scanning them.' },
  { m: '5.3', q: 'When an agent is approved, its permitted tools, data and actions are recorded. How does this record help detection later?',
    o: ['It lets the agent approve its own new permissions as needed', 'It acts as a purpose profile, a baseline for spotting misuse', 'It replaces the need to log the agent’s tool calls at all', 'It guarantees that the agent can never be manipulated'], a: 1,
    why: 'A purpose profile records the approved tools, data and actions, so activity outside it, such as a new tool or data source, stands out as possible misuse.' },
  { m: '5.3', q: 'A secret scanner finds 400 possible credentials in old repositories, including API keys for model providers. The team can’t rotate them all at once. What should it do FIRST?',
    o: ['Rotate the credentials in alphabetical order of repository name', 'Check which credentials are still live, and rotate those ones first', 'Delete the old repositories, which removes the need to rotate any', 'Wait for providers to report misuse before rotating any of them'], a: 1,
    why: 'Validating which secrets are still active focuses effort on real exposure. Deleting repositories doesn’t revoke credentials that may already have been copied, and waiting for misuse is too late.' },
  { m: '5.4', q: 'Which vulnerability prioritisation method uses a decision tree of exploitation status, exposure and impact, ending in outcomes such as “track” or “act”?',
    o: ['CVSS, which calculates a numeric base severity score', 'SSVC, Stakeholder-Specific Vulnerability Categorization', 'EPSS, which estimates the probability of exploitation', 'KEV, which lists vulnerabilities known to be exploited'], a: 1,
    why: 'SSVC (Stakeholder-Specific Vulnerability Categorization) walks a decision tree to reach track, track*, attend or act, reflecting context.' },
  { m: '5.4', q: 'Which open resource catalogues AI failure modes and vulnerabilities, such as model behaviours, rather than only software flaws?',
    o: ['The National Vulnerability Database’s list of software CVE entries', 'AVID, a community-run database focused on AI-specific problems', 'The CIS Benchmarks for hardening operating systems and clouds', 'The OWASP Top 10 list of the most critical web application risks'], a: 1,
    why: 'AVID is an open knowledge base of AI failure modes and vulnerabilities, complementing CVE-based databases for software flaws.' },
  { m: '5.4', q: 'A medium-severity flaw on an internal chatbot lets an attacker reach an over-privileged service account with access to customer data. Why does this raise its priority?',
    o: ['Internal chatbots are always the systems that attackers target first', 'It forms an attack path from the flaw through to sensitive data', 'Medium-severity flaws are automatically upgraded after 30 days', 'Service accounts are excluded from vulnerability management'], a: 1,
    why: 'Weaknesses that chain into an attack path to critical assets deserve higher priority than their individual scores suggest.' },
  { m: '5.4', q: 'Which assessment ranks how critical each AI-supported activity is, based on the consequences of disrupting it, to guide vulnerability priorities and recovery targets?',
    o: ['A penetration test of the organisation’s external network', 'A business impact analysis, completed with process owners', 'A code review of the application that hosts each model', 'A user satisfaction survey covering all of the AI tools in use'], a: 1,
    why: 'A business impact analysis assesses the consequences of disrupting each process, ranking criticality for prioritisation and continuity planning.' },
  { m: '5.5', q: 'An AI incident team lists its response options in order, from tightening guardrails through throttling to taking the system offline. What is this called?',
    o: ['A risk register, which records risks with owners and ratings', 'A containment ladder, choosing the lightest effective action', 'A kill chain, which models the stages of an attack', 'A RACI matrix, which assigns roles for each activity'], a: 1,
    why: 'A containment ladder orders options from least to most disruptive, so responders can choose the lightest action that stops the harm.' },
  { m: '5.5', q: 'An AI chatbot is revealing other customers’ data in its answers. Which containment step is MOST appropriate FIRST?',
    o: ['Retrain the model on a cleaned dataset over the coming month', 'Restrict the feature or take it offline, while preserving evidence', 'Post an apology on social media before investigating further', 'Wait for more customers to report the problem to confirm it'], a: 1,
    why: 'Active data exposure needs immediate containment, with evidence preserved for investigation and notification decisions.' },
  { m: '5.5', q: 'During an AI incident, responders collected logs, model files and prompts, but later can’t show who collected each item, when, or that it is unchanged. Which record would BEST have prevented this?',
    o: ['A daily status email summarising the progress of the investigation', 'An evidence manifest with source, collector, time, hash and location', 'A copy of the incident response plan, stored alongside the evidence', 'Screenshots of each system, taken at the end of the investigation'], a: 1,
    why: 'An evidence manifest records each preserved item with its source, collector, time, hash and storage location, supporting the chain of custody and the integrity of the evidence.' },
  { m: '5.5', q: 'An AI incident has just been declared. Routine jobs will delete older prompt logs and superseded model versions overnight. What should responders do FIRST?',
    o: ['Let the jobs run, since only older data is going to be removed', 'Apply retention locks, so evidence isn’t deleted mid-investigation', 'Turn off all logging, so new records don’t overwrite the older ones', 'Ask the vendor to keep a copy of anything that it happens to hold'], a: 1,
    why: 'Preserving evidence comes before routine housekeeping. Retention locks or legal holds suspend deletion of logs, datasets and model versions the investigation may need. Turning off logging would lose future evidence.' },
  { m: '5.5', q: 'A hijacked agent changed delivery addresses on 300 customer orders before it was stopped. Which step BEST deals with the changes already made?',
    o: ['Leave the changes in place, since the agent has now been stopped', 'Compensating transactions that restore the verified previous values', 'Delete the 300 orders, so that customers can place them again', 'Retrain the agent, so that it reverses the changes on its own'], a: 1,
    why: 'Compensating transactions reverse the effects of earlier actions, such as restoring records, using verified previous values and proper approval.' },
  { m: '5.6', q: 'Which intrusion analysis model links four features of each event: adversary, capability, infrastructure and victim?',
    o: ['The Cyber Kill Chain, which orders the stages of an intrusion', 'The Diamond Model, used to pivot between related intrusions', 'STRIDE, which classifies threats into six distinct categories', 'The five whys, which drills down to an underlying cause'], a: 1,
    why: 'The Diamond Model links adversary, capability, infrastructure and victim, helping analysts pivot between related activity.' },
  { m: '5.6', q: 'During root cause analysis, a team keeps asking why each failure happened until it reaches a cause that, if fixed, would prevent recurrence. Which technique is this?',
    o: ['A fishbone diagram, which groups causes into broad categories', 'The five whys, an iterative questioning method for root causes', 'Dataset diffing, which compares versions of a training dataset', 'Influence analysis, which links model behaviour to training data'], a: 1,
    why: 'The five whys technique repeats the question until it reaches a root cause whose fix would prevent recurrence.' },
  { m: '5.6', q: 'Investigators suspect that poisoned records entered a training dataset between two versions. Which technique MOST directly finds them?',
    o: ['Rerunning the model on the same prompts used during the incident', 'Dataset diffing, comparing the before and after datasets', 'Raising the model’s temperature to expose hidden behaviour', 'Reviewing the model card for any mention of the dataset'], a: 1,
    why: 'Dataset diffing compares versions to find records that were added, removed or altered, narrowing the search for poisoned data.' },
  { m: '5.6', q: 'An agent forwarded confidential files after reading an email containing hidden instructions. The team has disabled the agent’s email tool. Which further step is MOST important to stop the attack recurring?',
    o: ['Retrain the underlying model on a larger and more recent dataset', 'Find and remove the carrier message, and any copies of it elsewhere', 'Reset every employee’s password across the whole organisation', 'Share the attacker’s instructions widely so staff can recognise them'], a: 1,
    why: 'In indirect injection, the carrier is the content that delivered the instructions. Until every copy is found and removed, including indexed or cached copies, any assistant that reads it could be compromised again. Retraining and mass password resets don’t remove it.' },
  { m: '5.7', q: 'A claims process can tolerate at most eight hours of disruption. Its AI triage service must be restored and then checked before claims can flow again. How should the service’s recovery time objective BEST be set?',
    o: ['At exactly eight hours, to match the maximum tolerable downtime', 'Well under eight hours, leaving time for checks and dependent steps', 'At twelve hours, since some manual processing would continue anyway', 'At twenty-four hours, in line with the provider’s standard SLA'], a: 1,
    why: 'The RTO must be shorter than the maximum tolerable downtime, leaving room for validation and any dependent steps before the business process resumes. Matching it exactly leaves no margin at all.' },
  { m: '5.7', q: 'After restoring a vector store from a snapshot, which check BEST confirms that retrieval works as expected before it returns to production?',
    o: ['Checking that the database service starts without errors', 'Running golden queries that have known correct results', 'Asking a few users whether its answers seem better', 'Comparing the store’s size with the size before restore'], a: 1,
    why: 'Golden queries have known correct results, so they verify that a restored or changed index returns the right content.' },
  { m: '5.7', q: 'An organisation wants to publish lessons from an AI incident so that anyone may share them freely. Which Traffic Light Protocol label applies?',
    o: ['TLP:RED, limited to the people present when it is shared', 'TLP:CLEAR, which sets no limits on any further disclosure', 'TLP:AMBER, shared within recipients’ organisations as needed', 'TLP:GREEN, shared within the recipients’ wider community'], a: 1,
    why: 'TLP:CLEAR means the information may be shared without restriction. RED, AMBER and GREEN progressively limit who may receive it.' },
  { m: '5.7', q: 'A circuit breaker on an AI service trips because harmful outputs exceed a threshold. What happens next?',
    o: ['The service keeps serving harmful outputs until a person decides', 'Traffic moves to a safe fallback, then recovers via a half-open state', 'The model is permanently deleted, and then has to be rebuilt from scratch', 'All users are signed out at once, and their accounts are disabled'], a: 1,
    why: 'A tripped breaker routes traffic to a fallback, then tests recovery in a half-open state before fully restoring service.' },
  { m: '6.1', q: 'A team must threat model a health assistant for risks such as users being linked across sessions or identified from their data. Which method fits BEST?',
    o: ['STRIDE, which focuses on six categories of security threats', 'LINDDUN, a method designed for privacy threat modelling', 'DREAD, which scores threats against five rating factors', 'PASTA, which links threats to business impact in stages'], a: 1,
    why: 'LINDDUN covers privacy threats: linking, identifying, non-repudiation, detecting, data disclosure, unawareness and non-compliance.' },
  { m: '6.1', q: 'Attackers craft adversarial inputs against their own open-source copy of a similar model, then use them successfully against a company’s private model. What is this?',
    o: ['A white-box attack, using the target’s own weights directly', 'A transfer attack, exploiting similarities between models', 'A membership inference attack, revealing training records', 'A sponge attack, maximising the target model’s computation'], a: 1,
    why: 'Transfer attacks develop adversarial inputs on a proxy model and reuse them against a different target, so hiding a model’s weights isn’t a complete defence.' },
  { m: '6.1', q: 'Attackers publish authoritative-looking pages so that a research assistant cites them as sources for false claims. What is this threat called?',
    o: ['Typosquatting, registering look-alike names for popular packages', 'Citation spoofing, a growing form of retrieval manipulation', 'Model inversion, reconstructing training data from outputs', 'Context stuffing, filling requests to drive up cost and latency'], a: 1,
    why: 'Citation spoofing plants sources that give false authority to an AI system’s answers. Source allow-lists and verification reduce the risk.' },
  { m: '6.1', q: 'A threat modeller breaks the goal “steal the model’s weights” into the alternative steps and sub-goals that could achieve it. Which technique is this?',
    o: ['A data flow diagram, showing components, flows and stores', 'An attack tree, with the attacker’s objective at its root', 'A risk register, recording owners, ratings and treatments', 'A RACI matrix, assigning roles for each decision'], a: 1,
    why: 'Attack trees decompose an attacker’s goal into alternative sub-goals and steps, showing which paths are cheapest or least defended.' },
  { m: '6.1', q: 'An insider who knows a model’s architecture and has its weights crafts adversarial inputs using its gradients. What type of attack is this?',
    o: ['A black-box attack, using only the model’s public outputs', 'A white-box attack, using full knowledge of the model', 'A transfer attack, using a different proxy model', 'A denial-of-wallet attack, driving up compute costs'], a: 1,
    why: 'White-box attacks use knowledge of the architecture and weights, such as gradients, making them the strongest form of adversarial attack.' },
  { m: '6.1', q: 'A team keeps its AI system’s threat model in versioned, machine-readable files that are updated and reviewed with each design change. What is this practice?',
    o: ['Security through obscurity, hiding the design from attackers', 'Threat modelling as code, using tools such as pytm or Threagile', 'Penetration testing, attacking the live system to find flaws', 'Configuration drift, where settings change outside control'], a: 1,
    why: 'Threat modelling as code describes systems and threats in versioned files, using tools such as Threat Dragon, pytm or Threagile, so models stay current.' },
  { m: '6.2', q: 'A new assistant version goes first to internal staff, then to pilot customers, and finally to everyone. Which rollout strategy is this?',
    o: ['A big-bang release, switching all users at the same moment', 'A ring deployment, widening the audience in planned stages', 'A shadow deployment, where users never see new outputs', 'A blue/green switch, moving all traffic between two stacks'], a: 1,
    why: 'Ring deployments release to progressively wider groups, limiting exposure while confidence grows.' },
  { m: '6.2', q: 'A newly released summarisation capability starts producing harmful output for a subset of users. The team must stop it within minutes, without a full redeployment. Which mechanism BEST supports this?',
    o: ['A scheduled release train, with the next deployment due in two weeks', 'A feature flag that can turn it off for some or all users at once', 'A code review of the change, before it goes into the next release', 'A user survey asking how often people use the new summaries'], a: 1,
    why: 'Feature flags separate release from deployment, so a capability can be switched off instantly for some or all users. Release trains and reviews take far longer and don’t stop current harm.' },
  { m: '6.2', q: 'A release migrates a vector store to a new schema. Why should the migration be designed for backward compatibility?',
    o: ['So that the new schema runs faster than the previous one did', 'So that rolling back doesn’t break or corrupt the system’s data', 'So that the vendor can support the old version indefinitely', 'So that every user sees the change at exactly the same time'], a: 1,
    why: 'Backward-compatible changes let the previous release be restored without breaking or corrupting data, keeping rollback a real option.' },
  { m: '6.2', q: 'Which 2025 ETSI technical specification sets baseline cyber security requirements for AI models and systems across their lifecycle?',
    o: ['ISO/IEC 27001, which certifies information security management', 'ETSI TS 104 223, derived from the UK’s AI code of practice', 'NIST SP 800-53, which catalogues US federal security controls', 'PCI DSS, which secures payment card data in organisations'], a: 1,
    why: 'ETSI TS 104 223 sets baseline cyber security requirements for AI models and systems, building on the UK’s AI Cyber Security Code of Practice.' },
  { m: '6.3', q: 'An AI writing tool’s vendor sends customer text to a foundation model provider that the customer has no contract with. What is this risk called?',
    o: ['Concentration risk, from many services relying on one provider', 'Fourth-party risk, arising further down the supply chain', 'Model drift, from changes in the input data over time', 'Insider risk, from the customer’s own employees'], a: 1,
    why: 'Fourth-party risk comes from your vendors’ suppliers. Assess it through the vendor’s contracts, sub-processor lists and assurance reports.' },
  { m: '6.3', q: 'Which MLCommons format provides machine-readable descriptions of machine learning datasets?',
    o: ['CycloneDX, a bill of materials standard from OWASP', 'Croissant, which can also record responsible AI details', 'Safetensors, a format for storing model weights safely', 'C2PA, a provenance standard for images and other media'], a: 1,
    why: 'Croissant is MLCommons’ machine-readable dataset metadata format, with responsible AI fields that support provenance and documentation.' },
  { m: '6.3', q: 'A team wants to use an open model released under a Responsible AI Licence (OpenRAIL-M) in a product for its customers. What should the legal review check MOST carefully?',
    o: ['Whether the model’s weights may be stored on the company’s own servers', 'That its use-based restrictions are respected and passed on to users', 'Whether the licence permits the model to be fine-tuned in any way', 'That the model was trained on a dataset above a minimum size'], a: 1,
    why: 'RAIL licences allow broad use but prohibit specified uses, and require those restrictions to be passed on in derivative works and to downstream users. That is what distinguishes them from permissive licences such as Apache 2.0.' },
  { m: '6.3', q: 'A security architect is assessing a cloud provider that shares GPUs between tenants. Research in 2025 showed that Rowhammer-style bit flips in GPU memory could wreck a model’s accuracy. What should the architect ask about FIRST?',
    o: ['Whether the GPUs are the newest model available from the vendor', 'Whether error-correcting code (ECC) memory is enabled on the GPUs', 'Whether the provider encrypts customer data stored on its disks', 'Whether tenants may choose the region where their GPUs are hosted'], a: 1,
    why: 'The GPUHammer research flipped bits in GPU memory and collapsed model accuracy. NVIDIA’s guidance was to enable ECC, which detects and corrects such flips at some cost in capacity and speed. Newer hardware, disk encryption and region choice don’t address it.' },
  { m: '6.4', q: 'Which artefact BEST reveals untested areas in a red team exercise, by mapping what was attempted across the system?',
    o: ['The rules of engagement, which set scope, limits and stop rules', 'A coverage matrix of technique classes against each component', 'The executive summary, which leads with the business impact', 'The vulnerability register, which tracks findings to closure'], a: 1,
    why: 'A coverage matrix maps technique classes against components, showing what has and hasn’t been tested.' },
  { m: '6.4', q: 'Testers are given an assistant’s system prompts, code, configuration and model weights before starting. Which kind of testing is this?',
    o: ['Black box testing, with no internal knowledge of the system', 'White box testing, with full access to internal details', 'Grey box testing, with some limited internal knowledge', 'Bug bounty testing, by external researchers for rewards'], a: 1,
    why: 'White box testing gives testers full access, including code, configuration and sometimes weights, enabling deeper analysis than black box testing.' },
  { m: '6.4', q: 'An automated red-teaming pipeline replays the same fixed list of attack prompts every night and no longer finds anything new. Which addition would MOST directly make its attacks adapt to the target?',
    o: ['A scorer that labels each of the target’s responses as safe or unsafe', 'An attacker model that generates and refines attacks using feedback', 'A larger database for storing the full transcripts of every run', 'A dashboard charting the attack success rate across releases'], a: 1,
    why: 'An attacker model generates new attempts and refines them based on the target’s responses and the scorer’s verdicts, as in PAIR-style loops. Scorers, storage and dashboards are needed too, but none of them creates new attacks.' },
  { m: '6.4', q: 'A company’s small internal red team tests its AI assistant before each release, but researchers keep publishing new jailbreak techniques. Which addition would BEST broaden its coverage?',
    o: ['Asking the internal team to repeat its existing tests more often', 'An AI bug bounty, bringing in many external researchers continuously', 'Disabling the assistant until every public technique has been fixed', 'Relying on the model provider’s testing instead of internal testing'], a: 1,
    why: 'A bug bounty adds many external researchers with diverse techniques, testing continuously between releases. It complements, rather than replaces, internal red teaming.' },
  { m: '6.4', q: 'A red team is scoping tests for an agent that can approve supplier payments. Which test is MOST specific to the agentic nature of this system?',
    o: ['Checking the web interface for cross-site scripting flaws', 'Checking whether injected content can make it act without approval', 'Checking the model’s accuracy on a general knowledge benchmark', 'Checking that the login page enforces a strong password policy'], a: 1,
    why: 'What makes an agent different is that it acts. Tests must check whether manipulated inputs can drive high-impact actions, such as payments, around approval controls. The other tests apply to ordinary applications too.' },
  { m: '6.5', q: 'A company runs an email-reading assistant similar to the one hit by 2025’s EchoLeak flaw, in which a crafted email made the assistant leak data with no user action. Which control would MOST reduce the impact of a similar flaw?',
    o: ['Training staff never to click on links in suspicious-looking emails', 'Restricting outbound channels, such as links and images in its output', 'Making the email server’s spam filter far more sensitive to junk mail', 'Switching the assistant to the newest model version available'], a: 1,
    why: 'EchoLeak needed no clicks: injected instructions made the assistant embed data in links and images that were fetched automatically. Restricting what output can reach, and isolating untrusted email content, limits exfiltration. User training doesn’t help against zero-click attacks.' },
  { m: '6.5', q: 'A red teamer’s successful attack transcript runs to 40 turns, and the developers say they can’t tell what caused the failure. What should the tester do FIRST?',
    o: ['Send the full 40-turn transcript again, with more detailed notes', 'Reduce it to a minimal case that still reproduces the failure', 'Rate the finding as low severity, since it is hard to reproduce', 'Close the finding until the developers have time to study it'], a: 1,
    why: 'A minimal reproducible case isolates the essential trigger, which speeds diagnosis, fixing and regression testing. Long transcripts hide the cause, and difficulty explaining a failure says nothing about its severity.' },
  { m: '6.5', q: 'Which standard describes how a vendor should handle vulnerability reports internally, from receipt through to remediation?',
    o: ['ISO/IEC 29147, the standard for vulnerability disclosure', 'ISO/IEC 30111, the standard for vulnerability handling', 'ISO/IEC 42001, the standard for AI management systems', 'ISO/IEC 27005, the standard for information security risk'], a: 1,
    why: 'ISO/IEC 30111 covers vendors’ internal vulnerability handling. ISO/IEC 29147 covers disclosure: receiving reports and publishing advisories.' },
  { m: '6.5', q: 'Two findings have the same impact. One needs an attacker with internal network access and specialist skills; the other works for anyone with a free account. Which prioritisation is BEST?',
    o: ['Equal priority, since severity depends only on the impact', 'Higher priority for the one that needs no special access or skill', 'Higher priority for the one that needs specialist skills', 'Lower priority for both, until one of them is actively exploited'], a: 1,
    why: 'With equal impact, the finding that is easier to exploit, by more people, carries more risk and should be fixed first. Exploitability is a core factor in severity and priority.' },
  { m: '6.6', q: 'An agent can trigger actions from a fixed list but is never shown what they return, so their outputs can’t influence it. Which design pattern is this?',
    o: ['The plan-then-execute pattern, fixing a plan before reading data', 'The action-selector pattern, strictly one-way by construction', 'The dual LLM pattern, with privileged and quarantined models', 'The map-reduce pattern, processing items in isolated workers'], a: 1,
    why: 'The action-selector pattern lets an agent choose actions without feeding their outputs back to it, so injected tool results can’t influence later steps.' },
  { m: '6.6', q: 'A risk review finds that a customer-facing assistant can be tricked into revealing other customers’ order details. A full redesign will take three months. What should the team do FIRST?',
    o: ['Wait for the redesign, since interim fixes would waste engineering time', 'Apply interim controls, such as tighter access checks, and track the risk', 'Accept the risk until the redesign, without recording the decision', 'Turn off logging, so any leaked details aren’t stored anywhere'], a: 1,
    why: 'When the full fix takes time, interim compensating controls reduce exposure now, and the residual risk is recorded with an owner and a deadline. Waiting, or accepting the risk informally, leaves customers exposed.' },
  { m: '6.6', q: 'After turning a user’s request into a structured database query, an agent removes the original request text from its context before writing the answer. Which pattern is this?',
    o: ['Context stuffing, filling requests to the maximum input length', 'Context minimisation, keeping only what later steps need', 'Context budgeting, reserving space for key instructions', 'Spotlighting, marking untrusted content inside the prompt'], a: 1,
    why: 'Context minimisation removes content that later steps don’t need, so any injected text in it can’t influence them.' },
  { m: '6.6', q: 'Which technology embeds imperceptible watermarks in AI-generated images, audio and text, so they can be detected later?',
    o: ['C2PA, which attaches signed provenance metadata to files', 'SynthID, developed by Google DeepMind for generated media', 'Safetensors, which stores model weights without executable code', 'Croissant, which describes datasets with machine-readable metadata'], a: 1,
    why: 'SynthID embeds watermarks in the content itself, which can survive some edits, while C2PA metadata can be stripped. Neither is proof on its own.' }
] };
